latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Distinct Shapes:\n   - Iterate through the grid to find connected regions of the same color.\n   - Store each shape as a list of coordinates along with its color.\n\n2. Analyze Shape Characteristics:\n   For each shape:\n   - Calculate its bounding box (min and max row and column).\n   - Determine its vertical center within its bounding box.\n   - Calculate its "weight distribution" (proportion of pixels above/below its center).\n   - Identify if the shape is already vertically symmetrical.\n\n3. Determine Grid Characteristics:\n   - Calculate the vertical center of the entire grid.\n   - Identify the vertical order of shapes (based on their topmost row).\n\n4. Decide on Flipping:\n   For each shape:\n   - If the shape is already vertically symmetrical, don\'t flip it.\n   - Otherwise, consider flipping based on:\n     a) The shape\'s position relative to the grid\'s center.\n     b) The shape\'s internal weight distribution.\n   - The goal is to make shapes more balanced relative to the grid\'s center.\n\n5. Apply Flips:\n   - For shapes decided to be flipped, calculate their new positions:\n     - Maintain the shape\'s horizontal position.\n     - Flip vertically within its bounding box.\n     - Adjust vertical position to maintain overall vertical order of shapes.\n\n6. Reconstruct the Grid:\n   - Create a new empty grid of the same size as the input.\n   - Place each shape (flipped or not) onto the new grid:\n     - Use the original color for each pixel.\n     - Ensure shapes maintain their vertical order relative to each other.\n\n7. Validate the Result:\n   - Check that the number of colored pixels for each color remains the same as in the input.\n   - Verify that the vertical order of shapes is preserved.\n   - Ensure that no shapes have moved horizontally.\n\n8. Return the New Grid:\n   - Convert the reconstructed grid into a ColoredGrid object and return it.\n\nThis plan takes into account the nuanced approach required to selectively flip shapes while maintaining overall composition and balance. It considers both the individual characteristics of each shape and its relationship to the entire grid, which should lead to a solution that works for all the provided test cases and captures the essence of the challenge.'
